<!DOCTYPE html><html><head><title>IRC channel logs</title><style>html {
  background: #fdfdfd;
}

h1 {
  font-weight: 300;
}

h2 {
  font-weight: 200;
}

h3 {
  padding: .5em 0;
  border-top: 3px dotted #ddd;
  margin-bottom: 0;
}

form {
  width: 400px;
  display: flex;
}

input {
  width: 100%;
  display: flex;
  border-radius: .25em 0 0 .25em;
  border: 1px solid #aaa;
  border-right: 0;
  padding: 0.5em;
}

button {
  display: flex;
  border-radius: 0 .25em .25em 0;
  background-color: #007bff;
  border: 1px solid #007bff;
  padding: .5em;
  cursor: pointer;
  color: white;
}

button:hover {
  background-color: #0069d9;
  border-color: #0062cc;
}

a {
  color: #007bff;
  text-decoration: none;
}

a:hover {
  text-decoration: underline;
}

h4 {
  margin-bottom: .5em;
}

table td {
  padding: 0.75em;
}

table tr:hover {
  background: #eee;
}

.year {
  display: table;
}

.month {
  display: table-cell;
  padding-right: 1em;
}

ul {
  margin: 0;
  padding: 0;
  list-style: none;
}

.nick {
  padding-right: 0.6rem;
  font-weight: bold;
  text-align: right;
  width: 13rem;
  display: table-cell;
}

.nick a {
  color: inherit;
  text-decoration: none;
}

.message {
  display: table-cell;
  padding-left: 0.6rem;
  border-left: 2px solid #333;
}

.notice {
  color: #859900;
  font-style: italic;
}

.line {
  line-height: 1.8rem;
  display: table;
}

#logs {
  margin-top: 1.5rem;
  padding: 1.5rem;
}
</style></head><body><h1>IRC channel logs</h1><h2>2024-09-25.log</h2><p><a href="/bootstrappable">back to list of logs</a></p><div id="logs"><div class="line" id="013028"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#013028" label="[01:30:28]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">stikonas: thank you for making the tarball.</span></div><div class="line" id="013050"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#013050" label="[01:30:50]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">well, tarball is just make dist</span></div><div class="line" id="013100"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#013100" label="[01:31:00]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">not so hard to make :)</span></div><div class="line" id="013111"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#013111" label="[01:31:11]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">formatting release notes is a bit longer but oh well</span></div><div class="line" id="013134"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#013134" label="[01:31:34]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">lrvick: makefile doesn't really make sense for bootstrap-seeds (as you are supposed to be making your own if possible)</span></div><div class="line" id="013134"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#013134" label="[01:31:34]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I've also pushed new version into changelog.org file</span></div><div class="line" id="013204"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#013204" label="[01:32:04]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">stikonas: definitely appreciated</span></div><div class="line" id="013236"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#013236" label="[01:32:36]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Andrius Štikonas&gt; and also this time we haven't made individual releases (m2-planet, mescc-tools, etc...)</span></div><div class="line" id="013240"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#013240" label="[01:32:40]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">(post release changelog update)</span></div><div class="line" id="013345"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#013345" label="[01:33:45]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Andrius Štikonas&gt; but it's probably too late now</span></div><div class="line" id="013416"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#013416" label="[01:34:16]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Andrius Štikonas&gt; even if we make version bumps/tags, they won't be in stage0-posix-release</span></div><div class="line" id="013448"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#013448" label="[01:34:48]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">(my irc client temporarily lost connection, hence some out of order messages...)</span></div><div class="line" id="013453"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#013453" label="[01:34:53]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">well, they also haven't been moving eitherftfgbvhrdjeugvuijkgrrlkcebidnrneikurhnkehlevl</span></div><div class="line" id="013518"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#013518" label="[01:35:18]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&gt;.&lt; sorry small child attack</span></div><div class="line" id="013537"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#013537" label="[01:35:37]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">no worries</span></div><div class="line" id="013619"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#013619" label="[01:36:19]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">(and why I have been having a hard time finding time to make further progress)</span></div><div class="line" id="013637"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#013637" label="[01:36:37]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">well, that's understandable</span></div><div class="line" id="013705"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#013705" label="[01:37:05]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">stage0-posix is not in a bad state right now anyway</span></div><div class="line" id="013729"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#013729" label="[01:37:29]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">well, there can always be more improvements to M2-Planet / M2-Mesoplanet but oh well...</span></div><div class="line" id="014033"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#014033" label="[01:40:33]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">well, I want to do the M3 work but that is a bunch of complexity to do a GAS compatible assembler and linker; along with a C compiler capable of directly building TCC/GCC and without quiet focus time, I know I can't really make progress.</span></div><div class="line" id="014749"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#014749" label="[01:47:49]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yeah, gas compatible assembler and linker would be nice</span></div><div class="line" id="014817"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#014817" label="[01:48:17]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but yes, it's hard to work if you can only spare  a few minutes at a time</span></div><div class="line" id="014834"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#014834" label="[01:48:34]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">it takes some time to get into the state where actual lines of code are being written</span></div><div class="line" id="015029"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#015029" label="[01:50:29]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">one of the reasons why my progress with posix-runner is also very slow...</span></div><div class="line" id="072350"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#072350" label="[07:23:50]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">oriansj: yeah, totally makes sense, and I buy the xxd/sed logic anyway. I'll probably just vendor the seed source (with appropriate credit) in our stage0 on the next pass, then run through building it with several different distro containers then compare with each other that they all get the same hardcoded result hash, before we pivot to using the binary in an empty filesystem with just</span></div><div class="line" id="072352"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#072352" label="[07:23:52]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">that.</span></div><div class="line" id="072453"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#072453" label="[07:24:53]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">that way someone can quickly conclude either every distro we used to compare the seed is compromised, or the compiled seed by all of them, that matches the hardcoded hash we expect, is in fact built from the hex0 file</span></div><div class="line" id="072626"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#072626" label="[07:26:26]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">then we have our own standalone very minimal origin point for our distro that is very quick for others to reason about in-place</span></div><div class="line" id="072737"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#072737" label="[07:27:37]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">then use that stage0 container to pivot to a stage1 containerfile with the complete kaem scripts generated from live-bootstrap which also can be flat and fast to review.</span></div><div class="line" id="072815"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#072815" label="[07:28:15]">&lt;lrvick&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and our resulting binary hashes with this simplified path should still match the ones live-bootstrap gets, which we can also hardcode</span></div><div class="line" id="122328"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#122328" label="[12:23:28]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">lrvick: live-bootstrap in qemu/bare metal mode actually does build kaem-optional-seed from its hex0 source (the actual binary seed is instead builder-hex0 stage1)</span></div><div class="line" id="135946"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#135946" label="[13:59:46]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Andrius Štikonas&gt; Yeah, but we also cheat in builder-hex0 stage2 a bit, it is actually written in hex2...</span></div><div class="line" id="140022"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#140022" label="[14:00:22]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Andrius Štikonas&gt; It would be nice to sort that out</span></div><div class="line" id="142819"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#142819" label="[14:28:19]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I don't really consider that an issue - the hex0 code is technically generated, but it remains fully auditable; a rogue hex2-&gt;hex0 transpiler can't insert a backdoor without it being immediately obvious in the hex0 code</span></div><div class="line" id="184329"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#184329" label="[18:43:29]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Andrius Štikonas&gt; Well, basically the only thing rogue transpiler can change is insert jumps to another function...</span></div><div class="line" id="184351"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#184351" label="[18:43:51]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Andrius Štikonas&gt; Still, one can do similar things with kernels like with POSIX apps</span></div><div class="line" id="184425"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#184425" label="[18:44:25]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Andrius Štikonas&gt; First kernel can build hex0 apps and no advanced syscalls for later stuff)</span></div><div class="line" id="184441"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#184441" label="[18:44:41]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Andrius Štikonas&gt; Then of loads kernel that can understand hex1</span></div><div class="line" id="184445"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#184445" label="[18:44:45]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Andrius Štikonas&gt; And then hex2</span></div><div class="line" id="184459"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#184459" label="[18:44:59]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Andrius Štikonas&gt; After that it just continues all the way</span></div><br /></div></body></html>